// Chapter 4: a three-node cluster on Google GKE. The deployment sequence is the
// one chapter 1 taught, and what is GKE's own is small and said plainly: the
// operator detects GKE, treats it as a managed service whose kubelet it leaves
// alone, and carries one piece of GKE-specific advice, for the node pool whose
// local SSDs were assembled into a RAID array before the run ever saw them.
// The depth of the chapter goes to that node pool, to the two things a GKE
// install needs before the chart renders, and to what a managed kubelet costs.
TRAINING.register({
  id: 'gke',
  title: 'A three-node cluster on Google GKE',
  summary: 'The generic managed-Kubernetes path on Google GKE: a node pool with raw local NVMe, cert-manager and a persistent-disk class before the chart, and a draft whose environment turns the kubelet configuration off.',
  goal: 'you can create the node pool a GKE deployment needs, install the operator with cert-manager and a named coordinator class, confine discovery to the pool, read the refusal a wrongly built pool produces, and know what a managed kubelet changes about the storage nodes.',

  sections: [
    {
      heading: 'What GKE is to the operator',
      body: [
        'The person who commissioned this training asked for a simple three-node deployment into GKE, and the chapter adjusts that framing in one respect: the operator has no GKE path of its own. It detects GKE, and from then on treats it as one of four managed services, with Amazon EKS, Azure AKS, and Oracle OKE, whose provider owns the node image and the kubelet configuration. Everything else is the sequence of chapter 1 without the OpenShift parts. The chapter says so rather than inventing GKE features the code does not have.',
        'Detection reads two markers. The kubelet version a GKE node reports carries the suffix `-gke.`, as in `v1.36.4-gke.2046003`, and every node of a GKE node pool carries labels whose keys start with `cloud.google.com/gke-`, such as `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool` and `cloud.google.com/gke-local-nvme-ssd`. There is no API group Google registers, so the two node-level markers are all the evidence, and the OS image string `Container-Optimized OS from Google` is not read as one. The suffix is the build\'s rather than the service\'s: Google Distributed Cloud carries it too and reads as GKE, which a reviewer corrects in the draft.',
        { table: { head: ['What is read', 'Marker', 'Strength'], rows: [
          ['Kubelet version', 'the suffix `-gke.`', 'the distribution built the binary'],
          ['Node labels and annotations', 'a key starting with `cloud.google.com/gke-`', 'the weakest, anything with node access can write one'],
          ['API groups', 'none', 'GKE registers nothing of its own'],
        ] } },
        'Among the distributions the operator can conclude, OpenShift and Talos come first, then the four managed services, then K3s and Rancher last. A GKE cluster imported into Rancher carries both sets of markers and is concluded to be GKE, because GKE built the kubelet and that is what decides the host\'s shape. The Rancher finding stays on the record for a reviewer who wants the other answer.',
        'What the conclusion is spent on is one flag. A document whose `spec.environment` is `GKE` produces a cluster whose storage nodes leave the kubelet configuration alone, where a Vanilla or OpenShift document turns it on. The reason is in the code\'s own words: a managed service rebuilds the node image and its kubelet configuration for every node it replaces, so an edit made by a storage node is gone on the next node of the pool, and the images of EKS, GKE, and AKS keep that configuration outside the path the storage node rewrites, so the edit fails on the first node as well.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'The node pool: raw local NVMe or nothing',
      body: [
        'A storage node takes NVMe devices whole, by PCI address, and hands them to SPDK. On GKE those devices are the local SSDs of the node\'s machine type, and how a node pool attaches them decides whether the operator can use them at all. This is the one pitfall the repository knows about GKE, and the chapter spends its depth on it.',
        'A node pool created with GKE\'s `ephemeral-storage-local-ssd` option assembles every local SSD into a software-RAID array, `md0`, and mounts it as the node\'s ephemeral storage. The probe then finds each SSD a member of that array and held by the kernel, and declines it twice over: `RAIDMember`, a member of the MD RAID array `md0`, and `Busy`, the kernel refused an exclusive open because `md0` holds it. No device of the worker survives, the worker is declined for having none, and with every worker of the pool built the same way the run holds nothing to draft.',
        'A node pool created with `local-nvme-ssd-block` attaches the same SSDs as raw block devices, which is what the storage nodes need. The option is in the operator\'s own advice text, and the command below spells it the way `gcloud` does. The `count=` form is the CLI\'s, read from GKE\'s reference and not from this repository.',
        { code: 'gcloud container node-pools create simplyblock-storage \\\n  --cluster storage --zone us-east1-b \\\n  --machine-type n2-standard-16 --num-nodes 3 \\\n  --local-nvme-ssd-block count=2', title: 'A storage pool with two raw local NVMe SSDs per node' },
        'When a run on a GKE cluster fails because every device was declined and at least one refusal names a RAID member, the operator appends its advice to the failure, within the 1024 characters an event may carry. The run\'s message then reads, after the counted refusals: `GKE assembled the local SSDs into that array, which is what a node pool created with ephemeral-storage-local-ssd does; a pool created with local-nvme-ssd-block attaches them as raw devices instead`. The advice is added only for `GKE`, only when the draft has no node sets at all, and only when a device-level refusal carries the `RAIDMember` reason.',
        { callout: 'The fix is the pool\'s configuration and nothing on the node. Breaking the array on a node by hand would be undone the next time GKE replaces that node, which is the same reason the kubelet is left alone. Create a pool with raw NVMe, and run discovery again with a new OperatorOps.', kind: 'warn' },
        'Three nodes of that pool are what the chapter deploys onto. Three is the smallest count the default `1+1` stripe accepts, as chapter 1 explained, and a zonal pool puts them in one zone, which matters to the draft later.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Before the chart: cert-manager and the coordinators\' class',
      body: [
        'Two things a GKE install needs are decided before `helm install`, and neither is an OpenShift-style permission. GKE has no security context constraints, so nothing of chapter 1\'s first section applies. What applies instead is an issuer for the control plane\'s certificates, and a StorageClass for the coordinators\' volumes.',
        'The control plane serves TLS by default and cert-manager issues its certificates off OpenShift. The chart checks for `cert-manager.io/v1` while rendering and refuses to render without it: the refusal names the Helm commands that install cert-manager, and the alternative, `tls.enabled: false`. The ControlPlane carries a prerequisite flag, `installCertManager`, by which the operator installs a missing cert-manager itself, but that runs after the chart has rendered, so on the Helm path the chart\'s check comes first and cert-manager is installed by hand before the operator. With the default values the chart then creates a self-signed ClusterIssuer named `selfsigned`, mints its CA from it, and issues a client certificate for the operator, the two CSI plugins, Prometheus, and the FoundationDB peers, which is why mutual TLS can stay on.',
        'The coordinators\' volumes come from `spec.source.local.foundationDB.storageClassName`, and the class cannot be simplyblock\'s own, because the control plane has to exist before any simplyblock volume can. On Amazon EKS the operator picks a class itself: it installs the EBS CSI driver and a gp3 class when none exists, and records the choice in the ControlPlane\'s status. That choice is made on EKS alone. On GKE, a ControlPlane that names no class produces claims that name no class, so whatever the cluster marks as its default class provisions them, and the operator installs local-path-provisioner as a prerequisite it will not use, whose class it makes the default only when the cluster has none. GKE has one, so the deployment works by accident of the default, and the chapter names the class instead.',
        'The repository names no GKE class. `standard-rwo` is the name GKE gives its default persistent-disk class, read from `kubectl get storageclass` on the cluster rather than from the operator, and a deployment that wants another persistent-disk class names that one. In the chart the value is `controlplane.storageclass.name`, which the bootstrap template writes into `foundationDB.storageClassName`.',
        { code: 'kubectl get storageclass\nhelm repo add jetstack https://charts.jetstack.io\nhelm install cert-manager jetstack/cert-manager \\\n  --namespace cert-manager --create-namespace --set crds.enabled=true', title: 'Before the operator' },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Installing the operator: the chart, the ControlPlane, the driver',
      body: [
        'The chart\'s `standalone` profile renders the ControlPlane, the SimplyblockDriver, and the discovery run into the `simplyblock-bootstrap-objects` ConfigMap, and the operator applies them after it starts, as on every other platform. The values a GKE install states differently from chapter 1 are the TLS block at its defaults rather than the OpenShift provider, the coordinator class, and a discovery selector on the pool.',
        { list: [
          '**TLS** stays at the chart\'s defaults: `provider: cert-manager`, `mutual_enabled: true`, and the self-signed issuer the chart creates. The `openshift` provider is refused off OpenShift.',
          '**The coordinator class** is `controlplane.storageclass.name`, the persistent-disk class read from the cluster.',
          '**Discovery** is confined to the storage pool with `discovery.nodeSelector` on `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool`, and `discovery.enableControlPlaneNodes` stays off, because no GKE node carries the control-plane role.',
          '**`controlplane.local.eksRoleARN`** stays empty. It annotates service accounts for Amazon EKS\'s pod-identity webhook and means nothing on GKE.',
        ] },
        'The ControlPlane the chart writes is the one shown below, and an install without the chart writes it by hand. Its `installCertManager` prerequisite finds cert-manager already served and installs nothing, its `installLocalPathProvisioner` prerequisite is not consulted while a class is named, and the two EBS flags are consulted on EKS alone.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'Helm values for a standalone install on GKE', file: 'examples/gke/01-helm-values.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'profile: standalone', text: 'The operator hosts its own control plane. `managed` points at a control plane elsewhere, `empty` installs the operator alone.' },
          { at: 'provider: cert-manager', text: 'The chart refuses to render with this provider unless the cluster serves `cert-manager.io/v1`, so cert-manager is installed first.' },
          { at: 'mutual_enabled: true', text: 'Allowed with the cert-manager provider, which issues the client certificates. The OpenShift provider cannot.' },
          { at: 'cluster-issuer: selfsigned', text: 'The ClusterIssuer the chart creates with `createSelfSignedIssuer: true` and mints its CA from. An issuer of your own goes here with that flag off.' },
          { at: 'name: standard-rwo', text: 'The coordinators\' class, written into `foundationDB.storageClassName`. GKE\'s persistent-disk class, read from the cluster, and never a class simplyblock provides.' },
          { at: 'cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool', text: 'The label GKE puts on every node of a pool. The run inspects the storage pool and nothing else, and the entries of a selector are ANDed.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The ControlPlane', file: 'examples/gke/02-controlplane.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'name: simplyblock', text: 'The only name the operator reconciles, in its own namespace.' },
          { at: 'local:', text: '`source.local` installs FoundationDB, the object store, and the management API here.' },
          { at: 'replicas: 3', text: 'Three coordinators, the default and the smallest count that survives one loss. The chart writes 3 for `redundancyMode: double` and 5 for `triple`.' },
          { at: 'storageClassName: standard-rwo', text: 'Named, because the operator chooses a class on its own only on Amazon EKS. Unnamed, the cluster\'s default class decides and local-path-provisioner is installed for nothing.' },
          { at: 'provider: cert-manager', text: 'The CRD spells the providers `cert-manager` and `OpenShift`. The chart translates its lower-case `openshift` on the way in.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The SimplyblockDriver', file: 'examples/gke/03-simplyblockdriver.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'driverName', text: 'The provisioner name of every StorageClass the clusters write. Nothing about the driver is GKE\'s.' },
          { at: 'enableVolumeSnapshots', text: 'Installs the snapshotter sidecar. The snapshot controller it needs is a ControlPlane prerequisite the operator installs and never waits for.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Discovery on a GKE node pool',
      body: [
        'The run is an OperatorOps with the `Discover` action, and on GKE it is told which nodes to look at. `spec.discover.nodeSelector` with the pool label confines it to the storage pool, and `spec.discover.workers` names nodes instead. The two are exclusive, because a selector\'s entries are ANDed and two hostnames in one selector match nothing. The control-plane flag is left alone: Google\'s control plane is not a node of the cluster, no node carries the role, and the flag exists for compact and single-node clusters where every node does.',
        'The run records the distribution it concluded in `status.environment`, `GKE` here, and the draft carries it as `spec.environment`. Two readings on a GKE node pool deserve a look before the draft is approved.',
        { list: [
          '**Huge pages.** The probe reads the huge-page pools of each worker, and `minHugePagesSize` in the draft is the free huge-page memory on the chosen NUMA node of the worker with the least, in whole GiB. A node pool that was given no huge pages has none set aside, so the draft leaves the value unset, and each storage node then computes its own minimum. What a storage node does on a Container-Optimized OS host to set pages aside, and how a kubelet the storage node does not restart comes to advertise them as `hugepages-2Mi`, is host-side behavior this repository does not show, so the chapter does not describe it. What the repository does say is that a reservation made after the kubelet last looked is missing from the node\'s allocatable, and that an SPDK pod asks for `hugepages-2Mi` and stays `Pending` on `Insufficient hugepages-2Mi` when the node cannot offer them.',
          '**The zone.** Every GKE node carries `topology.kubernetes.io/zone`, which is the run\'s default failure-domain label, so every worker of the draft gets `failureDomain` from it. A zonal pool is one zone, and one shared zone is one domain, too flat for failure-domain mode, so the draft leaves `enableFailureDomains` off and says in its notes that the control plane would refuse to activate the cluster. A regional pool spread over three zones is the case chapter 7 covers. `spec.discover.enableFailureDomains: false` reads no topology at all and groups the fleet by hardware alone.',
        ] },
        { code: 'kubectl get oops gke -n simplyblock -w\nkubectl get cdc -n simplyblock\nkubectl get events -n simplyblock --field-selector reason=DeviceDeclined', title: 'Following the run, and reading a refusal' },
        'A run against a pool built with `ephemeral-storage-local-ssd` ends in the refusal of the previous section. Each device goes out as its own `DeviceDeclined` event, the worker-level reasons and the GKE advice go into the run\'s message, which is what `kubectl get operatorops` shows, and no draft is written. Nothing on the node is changed by any of this. A finished run is not repeated, so the pool is rebuilt and a new OperatorOps is written.',
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The discovery run', file: 'examples/gke/04-operatorops-discover.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'action: Discover', text: 'The only OperatorOps action today. The run is kept afterward as a record of what it found.' },
          { at: 'cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool', text: 'The pool\'s nodes and nothing else. `workers` would name them instead, and the two cannot be combined.' },
          { at: 'enablePartitionedDevices: true', text: 'Reports disks that carry a stale partition table. A device that is mounted, held by the kernel, or a member of an array is never reported, which is why this does not rescue a pool built with ephemeral storage.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Reviewing the draft and what the environment decides',
      body: [
        'The draft is the whole deployment as one document, and the review is the one chapter 1 taught: read `status.message`, check the three workers and their devices, set `cluster.name`, and set `approved: true`, after which the document is immutable. What a GKE reviewer reads with particular care is `spec.environment` and what it spends.',
        { table: { head: ['Environment', 'Kubelet configuration', 'CPU topology'], rows: [
          ['`OpenShift`', 'on, with the KubeletConfig and the pool roll', 'on'],
          ['`Talos`', 'on, through the Talos API', 'on'],
          ['`Vanilla`, `Rancher`, `K3s`', 'on', 'unset, off'],
          ['`EKS`, `GKE`, `AKS`, `OKE`', 'off', 'unset, off'],
        ] } },
        'With `environment: GKE` the cluster the document produces carries `enableKubeletConfiguration: false`, and the storage node\'s environment tells it to skip the kubelet. CPU topology is left unset, which the workload renders as `CPU_TOPOLOGY_ENABLED=false`. Both are defaults a reviewer can replace: a stated `enableKubeletConfiguration` or `enableCpuTopology` on `spec.cluster` wins over the environment, and admission no longer refuses a stated flag off Vanilla. Stating the kubelet flag true on GKE makes the storage nodes try to configure a kubelet they cannot write and fail, and with the flag off, CPU pinning holds only if the pool\'s kubelet already runs the static CPU manager, which the operator neither sets nor reads.',
        'The host OS is stated apart from the environment, in `spec.hostOS`, and the run fills it in from `os-release` when every worker agrees. The expansion spends the distro on one thing, whether the host is Ubuntu, and states a family only when the ID or its `ID_LIKE` names one the inventory knows. What the probe reads on a Container-Optimized OS node is not in the repository\'s fixtures, so the example leaves `hostOS` to the run.',
        'Approval creates the StorageCluster and one StorageNode per worker, and the phases are chapter 1\'s: the document `Draft`, `Expanding`, `Expanded`, the cluster `Pending`, `Creating`, `Provisioning`, `Activating`, `Online`, and each node `Pending`, `Provisioning`, `Online`. The coordinators are not on the storage nodes unless the control plane was placed there, so the one-at-a-time rule of a compact cluster does not apply, and `nodeProvisioningBudget` is what bounds how many workers are added at once. What the node agent does to a Container-Optimized OS host during `Provisioning` is not visible in this repository, and the chapter does not claim a reboot or its absence.',
        'Once the cluster `gke` in the namespace `simplyblock` is `Online`, its reconciler writes the StoragePool `gke-default` and the StorageClass `simplyblock-gke`, with the driver\'s name as its provisioner. A claim against that class is the first volume. The persistent-disk class the coordinators use is the control plane\'s alone.',
        { code: 'kubectl get cdc,stc,sn,sp -n simplyblock\nkubectl get storageclass simplyblock-gke', title: 'The objects after expansion' },
      ],
      examples: [
        { title: 'The reviewed draft', file: 'examples/gke/05-clusterdeploymentconfig.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'approved: true', text: 'The review gate. The document is validated on every pass and expanded on none until this is set, and it is immutable afterward.' },
          { at: 'environment: GKE', text: 'Detected by the run from the kubelet suffix and the node labels. It sets `enableKubeletConfiguration: false` on the cluster the document produces and turns nothing else on.' },
          { at: 'enableFailureDomains: false', text: 'The reviewer\'s statement of what the draft decided: one zone is one domain, too flat for the mode. Left unset, the draft\'s note says the same.' },
          { at: 'nodeProvisioningBudget: 1', text: 'How many workers may be in the node-add process at once. On GKE the coordinators are not on these workers, so the budget is what bounds it.' },
          { at: 'gke-storage-simplyblock-storage', text: 'The three nodes of the pool, by their Kubernetes names, which GKE builds from the cluster, the pool, and a suffix.' },
          { at: 'mgmtInterface: eth0', text: 'The interface the probe chose for the node\'s internal IP. The name on a Container-Optimized OS node is what the probe read, not a fact this repository records.' },
          { at: 'failureDomain: us-east1-b', text: 'Seeded from `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` on every group. A zonal pool has one value, which is why the mode stays off.' },
          { at: '0000:00:04.0', text: 'The two raw local NVMe SSDs per node, by PCI address. A pool built with ephemeral storage would have put none here.' },
        ] },
        { title: 'The first claim', file: 'examples/gke/06-pvc.yaml', notes: [
          { at: 'storageClassName: simplyblock-gke', text: 'The class the cluster wrote for its default pool. Its labels name the pool, and its parameters carry the cluster id and the pool name for the driver.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'Backups from GKE: a Secret, not a provider chain',
      body: [
        'A cluster\'s backup store is `spec.backup` on the StorageCluster, an S3 endpoint and bucket with credentials, and how the credentials are obtained is `credentials.source`. `Secret`, the default, reads a static access key and secret key from the Secret `secretRef` names. `ProviderChain` names no Secret and authenticates through the AWS SDK\'s default credential provider chain: an EC2 instance profile, environment variables, or a service account\'s IAM role, which the type calls IRSA on Amazon EKS and OIDC federation elsewhere.',
        'What the operator wires for that chain is EKS\'s alone. `spec.source.local.eksRoleARN` on the ControlPlane annotates the service accounts with `eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn` for EKS\'s pod-identity webhook, and off EKS the accounts carry no such annotation. Nothing in the code reads a Google identity or annotates anything for one, so on GKE `ProviderChain` means whatever the AWS SDK finds in the pod on its own, and the operator sets up nothing for it to find. A GKE cluster that backs up to a bucket uses `source: Secret` with a Secret holding `access_key_id` and `secret_access_key`.',
        'Admission holds the pair together: `source: Secret` requires `secretRef`, and `source: ProviderChain` forbids it. The deprecated `credentialsSecretRef` beside the block is refused too. Chapter 10 is where backups are taken and restored, and it starts from a store written this way.',
      ],
    },
    {
      heading: 'What goes wrong',
      body: [
        { pitfalls: [
          { symptom: 'The run fails with `no worker has a device this run would use`, every device `RAIDMember` and `Busy`, and the message ends with the GKE advice.', cause: 'The node pool was created with `ephemeral-storage-local-ssd`, which assembled the local SSDs into `md0`.', fix: 'Create a node pool with `local-nvme-ssd-block`, and write a new OperatorOps. Nothing on the node can be fixed in place, because GKE rebuilds the node.' },
          { symptom: '`helm install` refuses to render, naming `cert-manager.io/v1`.', cause: 'TLS is on by default with the cert-manager provider, and the cluster does not serve cert-manager.', fix: 'Install cert-manager with the commands the message gives, or set `tls.enabled: false` to deploy without TLS. The ControlPlane\'s own `installCertManager` runs too late for the chart.' },
          { symptom: 'The coordinators\' claims bind to a class nobody chose, and a `local-path-provisioner` Deployment appears in `local-path-storage`.', cause: '`foundationDB.storageClassName` was left empty. Off EKS the operator picks no class, the cluster\'s default class provisions the claims, and local-path is installed as a prerequisite.', fix: 'Name the persistent-disk class in `controlplane.storageclass.name` or on the ControlPlane. Volumes already claimed cannot move, so name it before the control plane installs.' },
          { symptom: 'The draft has no workers, or the wrong ones.', cause: 'The selector did not match the storage pool, or `workers` and `nodeSelector` were both stated, which admission refuses.', fix: 'Select by `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool: <pool>` alone, or name the nodes in `workers` alone. A run matching nothing fails rather than writing an empty draft.' },
          { symptom: 'The storage nodes fail while configuring the kubelet.', cause: 'A reviewer stated `enableKubeletConfiguration: true` on a GKE document, and the node image keeps its kubelet configuration where the storage node cannot write it.', fix: 'Leave the flag to the environment. `GKE` turns it off, and CPU pinning then depends on the pool\'s own kubelet settings.' },
          { symptom: 'An SPDK pod stays `Pending` on `Insufficient hugepages-2Mi`.', cause: 'The node offers no huge pages to the scheduler: a pod of an earlier failed add still holds them, or the pages were reserved after the kubelet last read its capacity.', fix: 'Check for a leftover `snode-spdk-pod-*` on the host, which the control plane deletes when it can. What reserves pages on a Container-Optimized OS host is not described in this repository.' },
          { symptom: 'The draft states `enableFailureDomains` off although every worker carries a zone.', cause: 'A zonal pool is one zone, and one domain is too flat for the mode. The draft\'s note says the control plane would refuse to activate the cluster.', fix: 'Nothing, for a zonal pool. A regional pool over three zones is chapter 7\'s case.' },
          { symptom: 'A backup store with `source: ProviderChain` cannot reach its bucket from GKE.', cause: 'The chain is the AWS SDK\'s, and the operator wires it only on EKS through `eksRoleARN`.', fix: 'Use `source: Secret` with a Secret holding `access_key_id` and `secret_access_key`.' },
        ] },
      ],
    },
  ],

  questions: [
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'From which markers does the operator conclude that a cluster is GKE?', options: ['The kubelet version suffix `-gke.`', 'Node labels whose keys start with `cloud.google.com/gke-`', 'An API group Google registers', 'The OS image `Container-Optimized OS from Google`', 'The presence of the `gcloud` CLI on the nodes'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'GKE leaves two marks: the build suffix in the kubelet version and the labels it puts on every node of a pool. It registers no API group, and the OS image is not among the markers read.' },
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'For which environments does a deployment config turn the kubelet configuration off by default?', options: ['`EKS`', '`GKE`', '`AKS`', '`OKE`', '`Talos`'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'The four managed services own the kubelet configuration and rebuild it with every replaced node. Talos configures its kubelet through the Talos API, so the flag is on there.' },
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'Why does a storage node on GKE leave the kubelet configuration alone?', options: ['GKE rebuilds the node image and its kubelet configuration for every node it replaces, so an edit is lost on the next node', 'The GKE node image keeps the kubelet configuration outside the path the storage node rewrites, so the edit fails on the first node', 'GKE forbids privileged pods', 'The operator has no RBAC on a managed cluster', 'Huge pages are unavailable on GKE'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Both reasons are the code\'s own. Privilege, RBAC, and huge pages are not what the flag is about.' },
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'Beyond treating GKE as a managed service, what does the operator do specifically for GKE?', options: ['Appends an advice sentence when a discovery run refuses local SSDs as RAID members', 'Chooses a persistent-disk StorageClass for the coordinators', 'Annotates service accounts with a Google identity', 'Creates the storage node pool', 'Installs Google\'s persistent-disk CSI driver'], correct: [0], explain: 'The RAID advice is the one GKE-specific piece of code. The class choice is EKS\'s, nothing wires a Google identity, and the pool and the CSI driver are the cluster\'s own.' },
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'A GKE cluster was imported into Rancher, so its nodes carry both sets of markers. What does a discovery run conclude?', options: ['`GKE`', '`Rancher`', 'Both, and the reviewer picks one', '`Vanilla`', 'The run is refused'], correct: [0], explain: 'The managed services precede Rancher in the precedence, because Rancher imports clusters it did not install and the distribution that built the kubelet decides the host\'s shape. The Rancher finding stays on the record.' },
    { topic: 'Managed service', cat: 'concept', text: 'A Google Distributed Cloud cluster carries the `-gke.` kubelet suffix. What happens?', options: ['The run concludes `GKE`', 'The draft leaves the kubelet configuration off, which a reviewer can override by stating `enableKubeletConfiguration: true`', 'The run fails', 'The run concludes `Vanilla`', 'The operator refuses the draft'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The suffix is the build\'s and not the service\'s, so the cluster reads as GKE and its kubelet is left alone. The code says a reviewer corrects that in the draft, and a stated flag wins over the environment.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'action', text: 'Which GKE node pool option hands the local NVMe SSDs over as raw devices, according to the operator\'s advice?', options: ['`local-nvme-ssd-block`', '`ephemeral-storage-local-ssd`', 'A `pd-ssd` boot disk', '`enablePartitionedDevices` on the run', '`enableLogicalBlockDevices` on the run'], correct: [0], explain: 'The advice names `local-nvme-ssd-block` as the pool configuration that attaches the SSDs raw. The ephemeral option assembles them into an array, and no run flag undoes that.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'concept', text: 'A pool was created with `ephemeral-storage-local-ssd`. What does the discovery run report?', options: ['Each SSD declined as a member of the MD RAID array `md0`', 'Each SSD declined as busy, because `md0` holds it', 'Each worker declined because no device of it survived the device rules', 'The run fails with `no worker has a device this run would use` and the GKE advice', 'A draft naming the array as one device'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3], explain: 'Every SSD carries the two refusals, every worker is declined for having no surviving device, and the run fails with the advice appended. No draft is written, and an array is never a device the operator takes.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'action', text: 'What fixes a pool whose local SSDs the run refused as RAID members?', options: ['Create a node pool with `local-nvme-ssd-block` and write a new OperatorOps', 'Set `enablePartitionedDevices: true` on the run', 'Set `enableLogicalBlockDevices: true` and deny `md0`', 'Edit the draft to name `md0`', 'Approve the draft anyway'], correct: [0], explain: 'The fix is the pool\'s configuration, as the advice says. A busy device is never reported whatever the run\'s flags say, there is no draft to edit, and a finished run is not repeated.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'field', text: 'Which fields of `spec.discover` confine a run to the storage pool?', options: ['`nodeSelector` with `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool: <pool>`', '`workers`, naming the pool\'s nodes', '`workers` and `nodeSelector` together, for safety', '`enableControlPlaneNodes`', '`deviceFilter.pcieAllowList`'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The selector and the name list each select workers, and admission refuses the two together. The control-plane flag widens rather than narrows, and the device filter selects devices, not workers.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'concept', text: 'Does a run on GKE need `enableControlPlaneNodes: true`?', options: ['No, Google\'s control plane is not a node and no GKE node carries the control-plane role', 'Yes, because the cluster has only three nodes', 'Yes, because the coordinators run on the storage nodes', 'Only on a regional cluster', 'Only when `nodeSelector` is set'], correct: [0], explain: 'The flag lets the run consider machines that run the API server and etcd, which on GKE are Google\'s and not in the node list. Node count and coordinator placement have nothing to do with it.' },
    { topic: 'Node pool', cat: 'concept', text: 'Under which conditions is the GKE advice appended to a failed run?', options: ['`status.environment` of the run is `GKE`', 'A device-level refusal carries the `RAIDMember` reason', 'The draft has no node sets at all', 'The array is `raid1`', '`enablePartitionedDevices` is set'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The advice is computed only for GKE, only when a device refusal names a RAID member, and only on the path where the plan holds nothing to draft. The RAID level and the partition flag play no part.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'concept', text: 'On a GKE cluster without cert-manager, `helm install` is run with the chart\'s default TLS values. What happens?', options: ['The render fails, naming `cert-manager.io/v1` and the commands that install cert-manager', 'The operator installs cert-manager after the chart is applied', 'TLS is turned off silently', 'The chart installs cert-manager as a subchart', 'The ControlPlane waits in `Installing` until cert-manager appears'], correct: [0], explain: 'The chart checks the served API groups while rendering and refuses without cert-manager. The ControlPlane\'s own prerequisite would install it, but that runs after a render that never happens.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'field', text: 'Which chart values does the GKE install leave at their defaults?', options: ['`tls.provider: cert-manager`', '`tls.mutual_enabled: true`', '`tls.cert-manager.createSelfSignedIssuer: true`', '`tls.provider: openshift`', '`discovery.enableControlPlaneNodes: true`'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The three are the chart\'s defaults and the right values on GKE. The OpenShift provider is refused off OpenShift, and the control-plane flag defaults to off, which GKE wants.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'concept', text: '`foundationDB.storageClassName` is left empty on a GKE ControlPlane. Which of these happen?', options: ['The operator installs the EBS CSI driver and chooses a gp3 class', 'The coordinators\' claims name no class, so the cluster\'s default class provisions them', 'local-path-provisioner is installed as a prerequisite', 'The local-path class is made the cluster\'s default', 'The control plane refuses to install'], correct: [1, 2], explain: 'The gp3 choice is made on EKS alone. Off EKS and with no class named, the claims carry none and local-path is installed, its class made default only when the cluster has no default, which GKE has.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'field', text: 'Which classes may `foundationDB.storageClassName` name on GKE?', options: ['A persistent-disk class GKE ships', 'The class the storage cluster writes for its default pool', 'The local-path class, if the operator installed it', 'A gp3 class of the EBS driver, which the operator creates on GKE', 'None, the field is refused on GKE'], correct: [0, 2], explain: 'Any class that is not simplyblock\'s own, because the control plane has to exist before a simplyblock volume can. The EBS class is EKS\'s, and the field is optional everywhere.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'field', text: 'Where does the chart value `controlplane.storageclass.name` end up?', options: ['In `spec.source.local.foundationDB.storageClassName` of the ControlPlane', 'In the StorageClass the storage cluster writes', 'In the SimplyblockDriver', 'In the draft\'s `spec.cluster`', 'In the discovery run'], correct: [0], explain: 'The bootstrap template writes it into the ControlPlane\'s FoundationDB block. The storage cluster\'s class is written later, by the cluster, with the driver as provisioner.' },
    { topic: 'Prerequisites', cat: 'action', text: 'Which of these does a GKE install do before the operator that chapter 1\'s OpenShift install did not?', options: ['Install cert-manager', 'Bind the `privileged` and `anyuid` SCCs', 'Label the namespace for the `privileged` Pod Security level', 'Create a node pool whose local SSDs are raw block devices', 'Write the ControlPlane by hand'], correct: [0, 3], explain: 'The issuer and the pool are GKE\'s preparation. SCCs do not exist on GKE, and the chart writes the ControlPlane through the bootstrap ConfigMap on either platform.' },
    { topic: 'Install', cat: 'concept', text: 'On the Helm path, what does the ControlPlane\'s `installCertManager` prerequisite do on GKE?', options: ['Finds cert-manager already served and installs nothing, because the chart refused to render without it', 'Installs cert-manager during the ControlPlane\'s install', 'Replaces the chart\'s self-signed issuer', 'Is refused off OpenShift', 'Turns mutual TLS off'], correct: [0], explain: 'Each prerequisite is detected first and installed only when missing. On the Helm path cert-manager is there before the operator starts, so the flag finds it present.' },
    { topic: 'Install', cat: 'field', text: 'A GKE ControlPlane keeps mutual TLS on. Which statements are true?', options: ['Mutual TLS is allowed with the cert-manager provider', 'The chart issues the client certificates for the operator, the CSI plugins, Prometheus, and the FoundationDB peers', 'Mutual TLS has to be off on GKE', 'The OpenShift provider can be used on GKE', 'TLS has to be off because GKE has no issuer'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Only cert-manager can issue client certificates, and the chart issues every one the deployment needs, which is why the default is on. The OpenShift provider is refused off OpenShift, and cert-manager is the issuer.' },
    { topic: 'Install', cat: 'field', text: 'Which of these should a GKE ControlPlane state?', options: ['`eksRoleARN`', '`tls.provider: OpenShift`', '`source.managed`', '`foundationDB.replicas: 5`', '`prerequisites.installEBSCSIDriver: false`'], correct: [], explain: 'None. The role ARN is EKS\'s, the OpenShift provider is OpenShift\'s, the control plane is hosted here, three coordinators is the default, and the EBS flags are consulted on EKS alone.' },
    { topic: 'Install', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which of these does the operator change on the GKE node pool itself?', options: ['The kubelet configuration', 'The node labels', 'The RAID array of a pool built with ephemeral storage', 'The machine type', 'The OS image'], correct: [], explain: 'None. The kubelet is left alone on a managed service, the labels are GKE\'s, and the array, the machine type, and the image are the pool\'s configuration, which only a new pool changes.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'What does a run on GKE record about the environment?', options: ['`status.environment: GKE` on the OperatorOps', '`spec.environment: GKE` on the draft it writes', 'The node pool\'s name in its status', 'The zone in `status.zone`', 'The machine type of the workers'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The run concludes the distribution once and the draft carries it. The pool, the zone, and the machine type are read as labels for other purposes and not recorded as such.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'The storage pool was given no huge pages. What does the draft say about `minHugePagesSize`?', options: ['It is left unset', 'Each storage node then computes its own minimum', 'The run fails', 'The draft states `0`', 'The reviewer has to state a value before approving'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The value is the least free huge-page memory across the workers, and a worker with none or under 1 GiB leaves it unset, after which each node computes its own. Nothing fails, and the field is optional.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Every node of a zonal GKE pool carries `topology.kubernetes.io/zone` with the same value. What does the draft do?', options: ['Gives every worker that zone as its `failureDomain`', 'Leaves `enableFailureDomains` off, with a note that one zone is too flat for the mode', 'Fails the run', 'Turns failure-domain mode on', 'Requires `failureDomainLabel` to be named'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The zone label is the default failure-domain label and every worker carries it, so the domains are read. One shared zone is one domain, which the control plane would refuse to activate, so the mode stays off with a note.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'field', text: 'What does `spec.discover.enableFailureDomains: false` do?', options: ['Reads no topology at all and groups the fleet by hardware alone', 'Removes the zone labels from the nodes', 'Makes the run fail on a regional pool', 'Turns the stripe to `1+0`', 'Is refused on GKE'], correct: [0], explain: 'Stated off, the run reads no failure domains and the draft marks the cluster `enableFailureDomains: false`. Nothing on the nodes changes.' },
    { topic: 'Discovery', cat: 'concept', text: 'Where does the reason for a refused run show?', options: ['In the run\'s message, which `kubectl get operatorops` shows', 'As one `DeviceDeclined` event per declined device', 'In the draft\'s `status.message`', 'In the Node\'s conditions', 'In the ControlPlane\'s status'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Device refusals go out as events and the worker-level reasons, with the advice, into the message. No draft exists to carry a message, and neither the Node nor the ControlPlane is touched.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'field', text: 'What does `spec.environment: GKE` set on the cluster a document produces?', options: ['`enableKubeletConfiguration: false`', '`enableCpuTopology: true`', 'The `openshift` block', '`ubuntuHost: true`', 'Failure-domain mode'], correct: [0], explain: 'The managed-service case of the expansion states the kubelet flag off and nothing else. CPU topology is turned on for OpenShift and Talos only, the OpenShift block needs `OpenShift`, the host OS comes from `hostOS`, and failure domains from the topology.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'field', text: 'A reviewer states `enableKubeletConfiguration: true` on a GKE document. What happens?', options: ['The storage nodes try to configure a kubelet they cannot write and fail', 'Admission refuses the document', 'The environment is changed to `Vanilla`', 'GKE applies the configuration on the next node', 'Nothing, the environment wins over a stated flag'], correct: [0], explain: 'A stated flag replaces what the environment decides, and admission no longer refuses it off Vanilla. The code warns that stating it true where the kubelet is not writable makes the storage nodes fail to configure it.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about `spec.hostOS` on a GKE document are true?', options: ['The expansion spends `distro` only on whether the host is Ubuntu', 'A family is stated only when the ID or its `ID_LIKE` names one the inventory knows', 'The expansion refuses a distro it does not know', 'GKE nodes are treated as Ubuntu', '`hostOS` is required on a GKE document'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The distro decides `ubuntuHost` and nothing else, and the family is concluded from known IDs or left unstated. An unknown distro is carried, not refused, and the block is optional.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'Which statements about the draft on GKE are true?', options: ['It is validated on every pass and expanded on none until approved', 'It is immutable once approved', 'A reviewer can correct `spec.environment` before approving', 'A stated `enableKubeletConfiguration` on `spec.cluster` replaces what the environment decides', 'Approval creates the StorageCluster and one StorageNode per worker'], correct: [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], explain: 'All five hold, on GKE as anywhere: the gate, the immutability, the reviewer\'s correction of a conclusion that is a starting point, the precedence of a stated flag, and what expansion creates.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'After the cluster `gke` in the namespace `simplyblock` is `Online`, what does its reconciler write?', options: ['A StoragePool `gke-default`', 'A StorageClass `simplyblock-gke`', 'That class carries the driver\'s name, `csi.simplyblock.io`, as its provisioner', 'A StorageClass `simplyblock-simplyblock-gke`', 'A persistent-disk class for the coordinators'], correct: [0, 1, 2], explain: 'The default pool is `<cluster>-default` and its class `simplyblock-<namespace>-<cluster>` with the leading repetition collapsed, provisioned by the driver. The coordinators\' class is GKE\'s and was named on the ControlPlane.' },
    { topic: 'Deployment config', cat: 'concept', text: 'What is true of CPU pinning for storage nodes on GKE?', options: ['`CPU_TOPOLOGY_ENABLED` is rendered false unless the document states `enableCpuTopology`', 'With the kubelet left alone, pinning holds only if the pool\'s kubelet already runs the static CPU manager', 'The storage node restarts the kubelet to pin CPUs', 'GKE turns the static CPU manager on for every pool', 'The run reads the kubelet\'s CPU manager policy'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'The environment leaves the topology flag unset, which renders as off, and the code says pinning without kubelet configuration depends on the kubelet\'s existing policy. The operator neither restarts the kubelet nor reads its policy, and what GKE sets is not the operator\'s to know.' },
    { topic: 'Backups', cat: 'field', text: 'Which credential source does a GKE cluster\'s backup store use?', options: ['`Secret`, with `secretRef` naming a Secret holding `access_key_id` and `secret_access_key`', '`ProviderChain`, with `eksRoleARN` set on the ControlPlane', '`ProviderChain`, with a Google workload identity the operator creates', 'A GKE service account the operator annotates', 'None, backups are unavailable on GKE'], correct: [0], explain: 'The operator wires the provider chain through `eksRoleARN` on EKS alone and sets up nothing on GKE, so a static key in a Secret is the path. Backups are the cluster\'s and work wherever the bucket is reachable.' },
    { topic: 'Backups', cat: 'concept', text: 'What does `source: ProviderChain` mean off EKS, according to the code?', options: ['The AWS SDK\'s default provider chain: an EC2 instance profile, environment variables, or a service account\'s IAM role', 'The operator annotates no service account with a role off EKS', 'A Google workload identity is read instead of an AWS one', 'The operator refuses `ProviderChain` off EKS', 'No Secret is named, so no access key is stored in Kubernetes'], correct: [0, 1, 4], explain: 'The chain is the AWS SDK\'s, and the type names OIDC federation elsewhere as one of its sources, but the operator wires nothing for it off EKS. It is admitted anywhere, and it is defined by naming no Secret.' },
    { topic: 'Backups', cat: 'field', text: 'Which `credentials` combinations does admission accept?', options: ['`source: Secret` with `secretRef`', '`source: ProviderChain` without `secretRef`', '`source: Secret` without `secretRef`', '`source: ProviderChain` with `secretRef`', 'The deprecated `credentialsSecretRef` beside the `credentials` block'], correct: [0, 1], explain: 'Two rules hold the pair together: `Secret` requires the reference and `ProviderChain` forbids it. The deprecated field is accepted alone, and refused beside the block.' },
  ],

  tasks: [
    {
      title: 'A discovery run confined to the storage pool',
      scenario: 'Write the run that inspects the three nodes of the GKE node pool `simplyblock-storage` and no other node of the cluster, selecting them by the label GKE puts on every node of a pool.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: OperatorOps\nmetadata:\n  name: gke\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  action: {{action}}\n  discover:\n    nodeSelector:\n      {{key}}: {{value}}\n    enablePartitionedDevices: true\n',
      blanks: {
        action: { accept: ['Discover'], hint: 'the only OperatorOps action today' },
        key: { accept: ['cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool'], hint: 'the label whose value is the pool\'s name' },
        value: { accept: ['simplyblock-storage'], hint: 'the pool\'s name' },
      },
      explain: 'An `OperatorOps` with `action: Discover` writes the draft. `cloud.google.com/gke-nodepool` is the label GKE puts on every node of a pool, so its value selects the pool, and `workers` would name the nodes instead. The control-plane flag is not needed, because no GKE node carries the role.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The ControlPlane on GKE',
      scenario: 'Write the control plane the operator hosts on a GKE cluster, with its certificates from cert-manager, mutual TLS on, three coordinators, and their volumes on GKE\'s default persistent-disk class.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: ControlPlane\nmetadata:\n  name: {{name}}\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  source:\n    {{source}}:\n      image: quay.io/simplyblock-io/simplyblock:26.4.0-beta4\n      foundationDB:\n        replicas: 3\n        storageClassName: {{class}}\n      tls:\n        enableTLS: true\n        enableMutualTLS: {{mutual}}\n        provider: {{provider}}\n',
      blanks: {
        name: { accept: ['simplyblock'], hint: 'the only name the operator reconciles' },
        source: { accept: ['local'], hint: 'hosted here, not pointed at' },
        class: { accept: ['standard-rwo'], hint: 'GKE\'s default persistent-disk class, as `kubectl get storageclass` shows it' },
        mutual: { accept: ['true'], hint: 'the cert-manager provider issues the client certificates' },
        provider: { accept: ['cert-manager'], hint: 'spelled as the CRD spells it, lower case with a hyphen' },
      },
      explain: 'Only `simplyblock` is reconciled, and `source.local` hosts the control plane. The class is named because the operator chooses one on its own only on EKS, and `standard-rwo` is GKE\'s default persistent-disk class. The `cert-manager` provider can issue client certificates, so mutual TLS stays on.',
    },
    {
      title: 'The reviewed draft on a zonal pool',
      scenario: 'Complete the draft so that it is expanded into a cluster named `gke` whose storage nodes leave the kubelet alone, on a pool whose three nodes share one zone, and so that the operator starts.',
      yaml: 'apiVersion: storage.simplyblock.io/v1alpha2\nkind: ClusterDeploymentConfig\nmetadata:\n  name: discovered-gke\n  namespace: simplyblock\nspec:\n  approved: {{approved}}\n  environment: {{environment}}\n  cluster:\n    name: {{name}}\n    maxSubsystemCount: 50\n    vcpuCount: 8\n    stripe:\n      dataChunks: 1\n      parityChunks: 1\n    enableFailureDomains: {{domains}}\n  nodeSets:\n    - name: gke\n      groups:\n        - name: simplyblock-storage\n          workers:\n            - gke-storage-simplyblock-storage-7f3e2a1b-k2xz\n            - gke-storage-simplyblock-storage-7f3e2a1b-p9qw\n            - gke-storage-simplyblock-storage-7f3e2a1b-v4mn\n          mgmtInterface: eth0\n          dataInterfaces: [eth0]\n          failureDomain: us-east1-b\n          devices:\n            nvme: ["0000:00:04.0", "0000:00:05.0"]\n',
      blanks: {
        approved: { accept: ['true'], hint: 'the review gate' },
        environment: { accept: ['GKE'], hint: 'the enum\'s spelling, three capitals' },
        name: { accept: ['gke'], hint: 'the default pool and the class are named after it' },
        domains: { accept: ['false'], hint: 'one zone is one domain, too flat for the mode' },
      },
      explain: 'The document is expanded once `approved` is true. `GKE` is the enum\'s spelling and is spent on `enableKubeletConfiguration: false`, the cluster name decides `gke-default` and `simplyblock-gke`, and failure-domain mode stays off on a single zone, as the draft\'s note said it would.',
    },
  ],
});
